 "None of the above applies to you directly — there is no landlord to pursue. But the diagnosis half of this tool still works, and it is worth knowing that most damp reported as rising damp turns out to be something cheaper: a leaking downpipe, ground piled above the damp-proof course, a blocked airbrick, or condensation. Get the cause identified before anyone sells you a treatment.": "",
 "One caveat: this is a standard, not an Act of Parliament, so you cannot personally sue on the timescale itself the way you might sue on a breach of contract. What it gives you is a dated expectation and a regulator who is counting.": "",
 "One practical consequence: as a contract-holder you are not liable to pay rent for each day, or part day, that the dwelling is unfit for human habitation.": "",
 "Put it in writing today if you have not already. A phone call you cannot evidence is worth very little later.": "",
 "Reply to your own email so the original date sits in the thread. State how long it has been and ask them to confirm a date for inspection. Keep it short and unemotional — this is evidence, not an argument.": "",
 "Rust-red dust and white strands point to a fungus that travels through masonry and eats structural timber. It needs a persistent water source, so something is leaking. This is the one form of damp that gets significantly worse and more expensive the longer it is left. Report it today and use the word \"fungus\" in writing.": "",
 "Social landlords must also publish their response times and report their performance to the Welsh Government — so the numbers above are not just guidance, they are something a landlord is measured against.": "",
 "Soft, dark, crumbling wood in a wet area. Contained and fixable — but the water reaching it has not been stopped, so replacing the timber alone buys about a year.": "",
 "Something renters in England and Scotland do not have. Every private landlord in Wales must be registered, and anyone managing a property must be licensed. Licence holders are expected to comply with the law, and Rent Smart Wales can take action against those who do not. Mentioning that you are aware of this tends to concentrate minds.": "",
 "The April 2026 timescales above apply to social landlords only. If you rent privately, there is no fixed deadline — instead your landlord is bound by the continuous fitness duty covered above. That means they are not \"in breach on day eleven\" the way a Welsh social landlord now is under WHQS. They are in breach the whole time the home is unfit.": "",
 "The Welsh Housing Quality Standard was updated with effect from 1 April 2026 to set fixed timescales for hazards including damp and mould, for councils and housing associations. Where there is significant risk with likely imminent harm, the landlord must investigate within 24 hours and remedy within a further 24 hours. Where the risk is significant but harm is not imminent, they must investigate within 10 working days and remedy within a further 5 working days. If the work cannot be finished in that time, they must send you a written summary plan within 5 working days.": "",
 "The stages below are not legal deadlines — Wales has none for damp and mould. They are the points at which the next escalation route generally becomes available and worth using. What counts as a reasonable time depends on how serious the hazard is and who lives in the property.": "",
 "This is not optional and your landlord cannot contract out of it, cannot charge you for meeting it, and cannot make you responsible for it in your contract. If they have not given you a written statement of your occupation contract, that is a separate failure worth mentioning.": "",
 "This is the single most useful thing you can do, and in Wales it matters more than anywhere else in Britain — because with no statutory clock, the date of your written report is the only clock there is. Fill this in and copy it into an email.": "",
 "This is the strongest free route in Wales and the most underused. Councils assess hazards under the Housing Health and Safety Rating System and can serve notices requiring your landlord to act. Damp and mould score heavily, particularly where a child, an older person or someone with a respiratory condition lives in the property. It costs you nothing and you do not need a solicitor.": "",
 "Two months. If private, report the landlord to Rent Smart Wales. If social, your complaint should be through their process and ready for the Public Services Ombudsman for Wales.": "",
 "Two weeks with no inspection arranged is long enough to follow up. Reply to your own email so the original date stays in the thread.": "",
 "Under the Renting Homes (Wales) Act 2016, every occupation contract includes a term that the landlord must ensure the dwelling is fit for human habitation — at the start of the contract and throughout it. The Renting Homes (Fitness for Human Habitation) (Wales) Regulations 2022 list 29 matters and circumstances a court must have regard to, and damp and mould growth is the first of them.": "",
 "Water crossing the wall from outside through a specific fault: cracked render, failed pointing, a split downpipe, a slipped slate, a chimney flashing. It is a building repair and squarely a landlord repair. Say in your letter that it worsens after rain — that detail does a lot of work.": "",
 "Your local authority assesses housing hazards under the Housing Health and Safety Rating System, which treats damp and mould growth as a hazard in its own right. Severity is judged partly on who is exposed, so a household with a baby, an older person or someone with asthma or COPD scores higher. Where the hazard is serious enough, the council can require the landlord to act.": "",
